{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Natural and Forced Convection.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Natural and Forced Convection.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"natural and forced convection natural and forced convection","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Natural and Forced Convection.md","fileName":"Natural and Forced Convection.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Natural and Forced Convection","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 00:55\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Conduction Convection and Radiation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","Convective Heat Transfer Coefficient","Buoyancy-Driven Thermal Flow","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Natural and Forced Convection.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Natural and Forced Convection.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"natural and forced convection natural and forced convection","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Natural and Forced Convection.md","fileName":"Natural and Forced Convection.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Natural and Forced Convection","heading":"Natural and Forced Convection","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Natural convection arises when temperature-dependent density differences create buoyant motion without a fan or pump. Warm fluid tends to rise and cooler, denser fluid tends to descend, forming circulation that enhances heat exchange near a surface.\n\nForced convection uses mechanical motion to drive the fluid and can raise the effective [[Convective Heat Transfer Coefficient|convection coefficient]] substantially. A boundary-coefficient model may represent either regime, while a [[Buoyancy-Driven Thermal Flow|coupled flow model]] resolves the motion explicitly.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convectio